From 5b905cd3f6a290a69e862b42993091e92a004958 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hero <caleb@calebgj.io>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 22:50:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/7] lsa: D-LSA's index side as three libr4d ops, held to the
 paper's reference

D-LSA (latent sparse attention, Prefrontal-AI, arXiv 2610.42147) has a
full-attention decode query read its sinks, its window and the top_k keys
of the top_p buckets its index query scores best. This is the index side:

  lsa_index_store  a token's index key, normalize(k_free @ W_K), into a
                   bf16 KV group, and its index query (the group's query
                   heads summed) in f32
  lsa_cluster      each 512-key block, once it closes: pooled by 4 and
                   merged by Ward into 64 buckets, one KV page a block
  lsa_select       a decode row's buckets scored, the top_p taken best
                   first and walked to top_k keys, as an ascending list of
                   positions with the sinks and the window

libref/ref_lsa.h is the arithmetic, step for step the paper's torch
reference (reference.py build_buckets / read, ward.py), with each order the
reference leaves open fixed. lsa_ref_test holds it to the reference's
answers (lsa_golden.h, from tests/mklsagolden.py) exactly; on 56 fixtures at
the design's sizes (window 512, top_k 4096, top_p 1024; Gaussian keys and
keys through the published map) none of 1792 blocks' bucket sets and none
of 3584 reads differed, and 74 of 7.3M centroid elements by one bf16 ulp.
The kernels equal it to the bit on the host emulator (r4d_emu_lsa) and on a
card (lsa_device_test).

(cherry picked from commit 5cea97f09bb828021183014ad71a8a25036d9f20)
