+## Latent sparse attention (D-LSA)
+
+| op | params | operands |
+|---|---|---|
+| `lsa_index_store` | `M`(range) `head_dim` `n_head` `n_head_kv` `rot` `dim` `block_size` | `q`, `k`, `map`, `slot`, `cache`:inout → `iq` |
+| `lsa_cluster` | `M`(range) `window` `pool` `bucket` `cap` `dim` `block_size` | `pos`, `cu`, `idx`, `idx_bt`, `bkt`:inout, `bkt_bt` |
+| `lsa_select` | `M`(range) `window` `sink` `top_k` `top_p` `pool` `bucket` `cap` `dim` | `iq`, `pos`, `cu`, `bkt`, `bkt_bt`, `score`:inout → `sel`, `nsel` |
+
+D-LSA (latent sparse attention, Prefrontal-AI, arXiv 2610.42147) has a full-attention decode query
+read its sinks, its window and the `top_k` keys of the `top_p` buckets its index query scores best,
+instead of its whole history. The buckets come from clustering each `window`-key block of index keys
+once, when it closes; nothing is re-clustered. Three ops make the index side and an attention over a
+list of positions (`attn_paged_list`) reads it; `arch/common/rad_block_lsa.h` issues all three under
+`--lsa` (RadBuildCtx::lsa) and nothing without it. `libref/ref_lsa.h` is the arithmetic, step for step
+the paper's torch reference (`src/lsa/core/reference.py`), and libr4d's kernels equal it bit for bit.
+
+Two KV groups beside the attention's, both read over the whole history (`rad_kv_group_dense_read`):
+`kv_lsa_idx`, a token's index key as one bf16 row of 64 (declared at `head_dim` 32 over the manager's
+K and V halves, so the operand is `[blocks, 1, block, 64]`), paged like the attention's cache; and
+`kv_lsa_bkt`, one page a cluster block of `window` tokens holding that block's buckets, declared as
+bytes, `[blocks, 1, window, (64 + cap) * 2 / bucket]`: centroids `[window / bucket][64]` bf16, then
+members `[window / bucket][cap]` int16, block-local positions ascending, -1 past a bucket's size.
+`lsa_index_store` is the declared consumer of the first (its `block_size`), `lsa_cluster` of the second
+(`block_size` = `window`).
+
+### `lsa_index_store`
+
+Every row's index key and index query. The key input is each KV head's dims past its rotated prefix
+(`[rot, head_dim)`, after the k norm; partial RoPE leaves them as they were), the heads side by side:
+`n_head_kv * (head_dim - rot)` dims, 768 on Qwen3.8-27B. The query input is each group's query heads
+summed in f32 on the same dims, in head order, and rounded to bf16. `map` is
+`[2 * key_dim, 64]` bf16, W_Q's rows then W_K's, a raw pointer to the engine's upload (not a weight).
+Each input goes through its map as one fmaf chain per KV head over the head's rows, the heads summed
+in order; the product is rounded to bf16 (the bf16 GEMM output the paper's plugin has), then
+normalized as `x / max(|x|, 1e-12)`. The key is stored at the row's `slot` in bf16, the query written
+to `iq` in f32. A negative slot stores nothing.
+
+### `lsa_cluster`
+
+Every cluster block whose last key a sequence's rows wrote this step, clustered into its
+`kv_lsa_bkt` page. A sequence's rows (`cu`) close block b when `b * window + window - 1` lies in
+`[pos of its first row, pos of its last row + 1)`, so a rejected draft's rewrite of that key clusters
+the block again when the key is written next. The `window` index keys are summed in runs of `pool` in
+order; Ward merges the pools in rounds until `window / bucket` buckets remain: each live bucket finds
+its cheapest partner under `cap` (merge cost `|s_i| + |s_j| - |s_i + s_j|` over the key sums, from
+`(sq_i + sq_j) + 2 g_ij` clamped at 0, an equal cost to the lower partner), and the cheapest of the
+pairs that are each other's cheapest merge into their lower bucket, an equal cost to the lower pair,
+no more than leave the block its count. Buckets are numbered by their first pool. A bucket's centroid
+is its keys summed in position order over the sum's norm (clamped at 1e-30), in bf16. Every other page
+is left as it was. The grid covers `M / window` plus one block a sequence; a workgroup with no block
+leaves.
+
+### `lsa_select`
+
+Every query row's read, as an ascending list of positions. A row at position `p` sees the buckets of
+the `p / window` blocks closed before it, bounded by the table and by `score`'s width (the caller
+views the plane at the step's context bound). It scores each by `iq . centroid` (one fmaf chain over
+the 64 dims), takes the `top_p` best (an equal score to the lower bucket), and reads their members
+best bucket first, keeping those at or past `sink` and before the window `[p + 1 - window, p]`, until
+`top_k` are held; the last bucket is cut short. `sel` is the sinks `[0, min(sink, window start))`,
+those keys and the window, ascending, -1 padded to its width (at least `sink + top_k + window`), and
+`nsel` its length. Two launches: a thread a bucket scores into `score`, then a workgroup a row finds
+the `top_p`-th best by a radix select, sorts the candidates best first by a key no two share, cuts the
+walk by a prefix count and sorts the expansion.
+
